ABSTRACT

A permanent human settlement beyond Earth requires more than conventional astronauts, engineers, scientists, and operators. As lunar infrastructure becomes increasingly autonomous, civilizational functions emerge at the intersection of engineering, artificial intelligence, ecology, economics, psychology, governance, and systems science.

This document proposes a taxonomy of 20 core civilizational functions and 4 speculative research tracks. Each function is classified by maturity — Established Discipline, Emerging Function, Proto-Function, or Speculative Track — and must mitigate at least one of five reference failure modes F1–F5 derived from a controlled-dynamical model of the settlement.

The central hypothesis is that a lunar settlement is a coupled socio-technical-ecological system whose long-term persistence is governed by the non-emptiness of a viability kernel (Aubin) under bounded control.

