1. WHY A LUNAR UNIVERSITY SYSTEM?

A settlement can possess functioning machines and still fail because knowledge is lost, maintenance becomes impossible, autonomous systems behave unexpectedly, resource allocation becomes unstable, humans cannot coordinate under isolation, institutional mechanisms fail, ecological feedback becomes unstable, or dependence on Earth becomes excessive.

The fundamental educational problem is not "how do we train astronauts for longer missions." It is:

\boxed{\;\text{How do we train humans to operate a civilization whose infrastructure is partially autonomous?}\;}

This is a control problem over a coupled state space, and the university is the institution that must produce the controllers.

Order of derivation (enforced):

F_1,\dots,F_5 \;\rightarrow\; \text{control objectives} \;\rightarrow\; \text{functions} \;\rightarrow\; \text{curriculum} \;\rightarrow\; \text{simulator} \;\rightarrow\; \text{falsifiable claims}.

A function is in the core taxonomy if and only if it directly mitigates at least one of F1–F5. Otherwise it is a speculative research track.

